+# Agent Note: Projection cache isolates plain-JSON violations per unit
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-19-projection-cache-isolates-non-json-units.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The persisted projection cache replaced a session's whole checkpoint through one `snapshotJsonValue(rows)` pass, and a single unit whose state violated the plain-JSON contract made the **entire** record unwritable. The write path is fail-soft (`flushSoft` logs and keeps the cache stale), so the failure was silent: every session's cache row stopped updating, and `session.list`'s cold path served no title projection for those sessions. After an environment restart, the sidebar history showed each session's workspace title (the cwd basename fallback) instead of the task title — the title only appeared after clicking the session, when the host's live registry snapshot supplied it.
+
+The concrete trigger was a third-party plugin (`dsh-context`) registering a `contextTimeline` projection unit whose initial state contains `undefined` fields (`model`, `provider`, `lastModel`, `contextWindow`, `pendingShadowedSeqs`). `undefined` is not lossless JSON, so the unit's row always failed serialization and took the title (and every other unit) down with it.
+
+## Decision
+
+`SessionProjectionCache.put()` serializes **per unit** instead of per record: each `(key → row)` passes `snapshotJsonValue` independently; a row that fails is dropped with a warning, and the remaining rows still land. A dropped key reads as not-yet-available (`cachedSnapshot`/`viewCheckpoint` skip it) and refolds from the log on the next fuller read — the same degradation a lost write already costs, now bounded to the broken unit instead of evicting the session's whole cache (titles etc. persist). When every unit fails the pass, the previous record is kept rather than replaced with an empty one.
+
+The fail-loud contract (throw on any non-JSON unit) was deliberately changed: it never actually failed loud in production — `flushSoft` and `putSoft` both contain `put` — and its blast radius (one third-party bug erasing title caching for every session) outweighed its diagnostic value, which the per-unit warning preserves.
+
+## Verification
+
+The cache suite gained two cases: a bad unit's row is dropped while a good unit's value still lands and is served by `cachedSnapshot`; and a checkpoint where every unit fails keeps the previous record. End-to-end probes reproduced the original failure (dsh-context mounted: `put` threw `not losslessly JSON-serializable`, cold `cachedSnapshot` empty) and confirmed the fix (write succeeds, title survives in the cold snapshot, `contextTimeline` isolated out), including the full host `session.list` cold path serving the title.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Fix the plugin (`dsh-context`).** It is a third-party bundle outside this repository, and its released 0.12.x still ships the `undefined` initial state; the host must stay correct in the presence of a bad unit regardless.
+
+**Fail loud unchanged.** Rejected: the failure was already effectively silent (fail-soft wrappers), and its blast radius — losing title caching for every session — was disproportionate to the diagnostic value, which the per-unit warning keeps.
+
+**Write a record with only the surviving rows but no guard for the all-bad case.** Rejected: replacing a good previous record with an empty one would discard stale-but-never-wrong data; keeping the previous record is the safe degradation.
+
+## Consequences
+
+`SessionProjectionCache` no longer fails loud on a non-JSON unit — it drops that unit's row and persists the rest, so a single broken unit (e.g. a third-party plugin's `contextTimeline`) can no longer silently stop title caching for every session. The per-unit warning keeps the diagnostic value the old throw provided, now scoped to the offending key. A dropped key reads as not-yet-available and refolds from the log on the next fuller read; an all-bad checkpoint keeps the previous record. Existing cache rows are unaffected (per-unit pass only shrinks future writes), and previously lost title rows heal on the next write of that session.
